 def get_sysconfig_scheme():\0A        \22\22\22Get the \22scheme\22 corresponding to the input parameters.\0A\0A        Uses the `sysconfig` module to get the scheme.\0A\0A        Based on (with default arguments):\0A            https://github.com/pypa/pip/blob/ae5fff36b0aad6e5e0037884927eaa29163c0611/src/pip/_internal/locations/_sysconfig.py#L124\0A        \22\22\22\0A\0A        def is_osx_framework() -> bool:\0A            return bool(sysconfig.get_config_var(\22PYTHONFRAMEWORK\22))\0A\0A        # Notes on _infer_* functions.\0A        # Unfortunately ``get_default_scheme()`` didn't exist before 3.10, so there's no\0A        # way to ask things like \22what is the '_prefix' scheme on this platform\22. These\0A        # functions try to answer that with some heuristics while accounting for ad-hoc\0A        # platforms not covered by CPython's default sysconfig implementation. If the\0A        # ad-hoc implementation does not fully implement sysconfig, we'll fall back to\0A        # a POSIX scheme.\0A\0A        _AVAILABLE_SCHEMES = set(sysconfig.get_scheme_names())\0A\0A        _PREFERRED_SCHEME_API = getattr(sysconfig, \22get_preferred_scheme\22, None)\0A\0A        def _should_use_osx_framework_prefix() -> bool:\0A            \22\22\22Check for Apple's ``osx_framework_library`` scheme.\0A\0A            Python distributed by Apple's Command Line Tools has this special scheme\0A            that's used when:\0A\0A            * This is a framework build.\0A            * We are installing into the system prefix.\0A\0A            This does not account for ``pip install --prefix`` (also means we're not\0A            installing to the system prefix), which should use ``posix_prefix``, but\0A            logic here means ``_infer_prefix()`` outputs ``osx_framework_library``. But\0A            since ``prefix`` is not available for ``sysconfig.get_default_scheme()``,\0A            which is the stdlib replacement for ``_infer_prefix()``, presumably Apple\0A            wouldn't